   "source": [
    "## 1. Project Setup & Problem Understanding\n",
    "\n",
    "So basically the client is a retail company and right now they're sending the same marketing emails/offers to literally every customer, which is kind of a waste of money since a lot of those people were never going to buy anything anyway.\n",
    "\n",
    "What they want: some way to predict, for each customer, whether they're likely to purchase or not, based on stuff like their age, how engaged they are on the app/site, and how often they've bought before.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a classic **binary classification** problem \u2014 the target column `Purchased` is either 0 or 1. If this works reasonably well the client can stop blasting offers to everyone and instead focus budget on the customers who are actually likely to convert."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "output_type": "stream",
     "name": "stdout",
     "text": [
      "imports done\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression, LinearRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import (accuracy_score, precision_score, recall_score,\n",
    "                             f1_score, confusion_matrix, classification_report,\n",
    "                             mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, r2_score)\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.set_style('whitegrid')\n",
    "pd.set_option('display.max_columns', None)\n",
    "print('imports done')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Python Fundamentals\n",
    "\n",
    "A few things the brief specifically asks for \u2014 using core Python data structures (dict, list, tuple, set) and control flow (for/while, if/elif/else) for something actually useful, before jumping into pandas/numpy for the heavier lifting. Doing this on a small hardcoded sample first so it runs standalone without needing the real CSV loaded yet \u2014 that's next."
